WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:04.980
There are some stars who've showed up onscreen
for just a handful of minutes, yet still managed

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.180
to snag acting's most prestigious prize.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:11.620
Often, these are supporting actors who steal
the spotlight in a few seconds, but at least

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:15.280
one leading man won his Best Actor award for
barely any scenes at all.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.980
Some popular films stand the test of time,
while others fade into obscurity.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.660
Sadly, Shampoo falls into the latter category.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:28.900
This 1975 satire tells the story of a hairdresser,
played by Warren Beatty, who's juggling multiple

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:30.980
women while trying to open his own salon.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:36.080
The movie was a major hit at the time, raking
in nearly $50 million at the box office.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.940
It also impressed the members of the Academy,
as it nabbed four Oscar nominations.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.780
However, the only one to go home with the
gold was Lee Grant, an actress who was on

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.920
screen for just around 18 minutes.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.940
Grant won the Oscar for playing Felicia, a
lonely housewife who's trying to keep her

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:54.090
adulterous husband's attention while conducting
her own affair with Beatty.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:59.180
Felicia is whiny, wealthy, well-dressed, and
simultaneously lustful and lonely.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.660
Even though she's only onscreen for about
17 percent of the film, it was enough to impress

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:04.520
the Academy.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.080
This was Grant's third shot at Oscar gold,
after getting nominations for Detective Story

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.840
and The Landlord, and she finally won the
prize that night.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:16.500
Sadly, despite her award-winning performance,
it seems Shampoo has been scrubbed clean out

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.100
of the pop culture consciousness.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:22.240
The Silence of the Lambs isn't your typical
award-winning film, but it shattered all Oscar

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.780
expectations by becoming the first proper
horror movie to win the Best Picture Oscar.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:32.530
It was released in February 1991, and 13 months
later, it somehow crushed all the more award-friendly

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:34.480
films released during Oscar season.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:39.560
Most importantly, it's one of only three films
that have won the big five Oscars: Best Picture,

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:43.530
Best Director, Best Screenplay, Best Actress,
and Best Actor.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:48.000
Crazier still, that Best Actor award went
to a man who was in the film for a mere 16

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.420
minutes… but every second he's onscreen
is absolutely electric.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.729
Anthony Hopkins beat the likes of Robert De
Niro and Robin Williams to win the Oscar for

00:01:55.729 --> 00:01:58.840
his turn as the sophisticated psychopath,
Hannibal Lecter.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.500
Years later, people still talk about his performance
and quote some of his most iconic lines:

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.340
"I ate his liver with some fava beans and
a nice chianti."

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:19.520
From the moment he appears on screen, Hopkins
radiates pure evil.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:23.520
While he rarely raises his voice, he commands
every scene he's in, and with his unblinking

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.970
eyes and perfect posture, he totally unnerves
audiences.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:32.190
Sure, Hopkins only shows up for 14 percent
of the film, but every death-rattling, bone-chilling

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.620
moment is worth its weight in Oscar gold.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.180
"Yeah...that was good.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:40.620
Pity about poor Katherine, though.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.940
Tick tock tick tock tick tock."

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.640
After earning a Best Actress nomination for
Rachel Getting Married, Anne Hathaway got

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:53.240
her second shot at Oscar gold with Les Miserables,
an epic musical set in 19th-century France.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:57.819
Hathaway shows up for a mere 15 minutes in
a movie that runs nearly three hours long,

00:02:57.819 --> 00:03:02.099
but while she only appears in nine percent
of the film, she won the Oscar for Best Supporting

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:02.600
Actress.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:07.980
Hathaway threw herself into the part completely,
losing a shocking 25 pounds and cutting all

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:08.480
her hair off.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:12.960
She even went so far as to study prostitution,
and hearing the sad stories of modern-day

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:14.800
sex workers took a major toll.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:16.600
Then there's the performance itself.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:21.020
Whenever Hathaway opens her mouth, she's singing
at the top of her lungs, and on top of hitting

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:24.170
all those crazy notes, she's also acting her
heart out.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.240
She's desperate and lost and trying to survive.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.880
She's angry and hyperventilating and falling
apart.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.840
Once again, she's doing all of this while
singing, and her rendition of "I Dreamed a

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:34.819
Dream" is absolutely devastating.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:39.620
In short, she definitely deserved that award
for breaking our hearts in just 15 short minutes.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:45.040
In the '80s and early '90s, Kim Basinger found
frequent work in films like Never Say Never

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:48.020
Again, The Natural, and Tim Burton's Batman.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:52.450
But in 1995, she took a break from acting
for a couple of years until director Curtis

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:53.720
Hanson tracked her down.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:58.860
Hanson was adapting James Ellroy's neo-noir
novel L.A. Confidential, and he thought Basinger

00:03:58.879 --> 00:04:03.180
would be perfect for the part of Lynne Bracken,
a hooker sex worker with a heart of gold who

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:06.300
also looks a lot like Hollywood legend Veronica
Lake.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:10.330
Originally, Basinger wasn't interested in
the part, as she was sick of playing characters

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:11.780
defined by their sex appeal.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:15.830
But Hanson convinced her to take the role,
and Basinger made Lynne Bracken more than

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:17.370
just a blonde bombshell.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:21.419
Even though Basinger is only in the film for
15 minutes, she makes the most of her limited

00:04:21.419 --> 00:04:25.589
time, sharing intimate moments with Russell
Crowe and telling off Guy Pearce with a powerful

00:04:25.589 --> 00:04:26.140
monologue.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:30.580
"I see Bud because he doesn't know how to
disguise who he is.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:36.120
I see Bud for all the ways he's different
from you."

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.560
It was a standout performance that caught
the attention of the Academy and earned the

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.720
star an Oscar for Best Supporting Actress.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:47.920
During the 2000s, Woody Allen kept himself
busy, directing at least one movie every single

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:48.420
year.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:51.380
Almost all of those films have been completely
forgotten by time.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:55.640
Vicky Cristina Barcelona has pretty much suffered
the same fate as the rest of Allen's films

00:04:55.650 --> 00:05:00.229
from that era, but there is one little detail
that's saved the movie from total obscurity:

00:05:00.229 --> 00:05:02.960
it features an Oscar-winning performance from
Penelope Cruz.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:08.320
In fact, Cruz won a truckload of awards for
her turn as María Elena, the fiery and suicidal

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:10.319
ex-wife of a womanizing artist.

00:05:10.319 --> 00:05:15.080
She only shows up for 15 minutes, but she
crushes it in every single one of her scenes.

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:19.650
Whether she's defiantly speaking Spanish,
wildly trying to shoot Javier Bardem, or making

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:22.879
out with Scarlett Johansson, Cruz is the star
of the show.

00:05:22.879 --> 00:05:26.430
Even though she was up against some major
competition from the likes of Amy Adams and

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:30.849
Viola Davis, it should come as no surprise
that Penelope cruised to victory thanks to

00:05:30.849 --> 00:05:31.820
those stunning moments.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.780
Alan Arkin is one of those actors who should've
won an Oscar long before he finally did.

00:05:36.789 --> 00:05:41.289
The man was nominated twice, and he had plenty
of other powerful performances in other films

00:05:41.289 --> 00:05:42.289
as well.

00:05:42.289 --> 00:05:46.999
Thankfully, Arkin got his well-deserved trophy
at the 79th Academy Awards for his performance

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:48.180
in Little Miss Sunshine.

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:53.080
In a movie filled with amazing actors, Arkin
stands out as the larger-than-life Edwin Hoover.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:57.460
He's a dirty old man with a foul mouth, a
fondness for drug use, and an incredibly grumpy

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:58.480
disposition.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:02.820
But while Arkin earns plenty of laughs with
colorful language, he also tugs on the old

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:06.629
heartstrings in scenes like the one when he
tells his tearful granddaughter that she'll

00:06:06.629 --> 00:06:09.600
never be a loser so long as she tries to accomplish
her dreams.

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:11.720
"You know what a loser is?

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:17.380
A real loser is someone so afraid of not winning,
they don't even try."

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.279
Even though he's only in the film for a scant
14 minutes, he proved he was definitely a

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:23.840
winner when he snagged that Oscar.

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:29.280
1976 was an incredible year for film and an
even better one for supporting actors.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.400
Network, Marathon Man, and Rocky all featured
powerful supporting actor performances, but

00:06:34.409 --> 00:06:38.749
when the votes were all tallied up, the little
gold statuette actually went to Jason Robards

00:06:38.749 --> 00:06:41.280
for his commanding turn in All the President's
Men.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:46.640
Compared to his competitors, Robards' performance
as Washington Post editor Ben Bradlee is incredibly

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:47.400
understated.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:51.800
He's a prickly perfectionist, an editor who
drives his reporters hard and callously edits

00:06:51.809 --> 00:06:52.809
their work.

00:06:52.809 --> 00:06:54.180
But the man's heart is in the right place.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:58.599
Sure, he's brash, but he just wants a solid
story, reporting that'll withstand the scrutiny

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:01.819
of everybody from Joe Schmoe to the president
of the United States.

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:06.300
"I can't do the reporting for my reporters
which means I have to trust them.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.400
And I hate trusting anybody."

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:15.500
Robards is only on screen for about 14 minutes,
but that was more than enough to impress the

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:16.120
Academy.

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:20.860
They say good things come to those who wait,
and that's especially true for Jack Palance.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:25.659
The Hollywood veteran was nominated for Best
Supporting Actor in 1952 for his performance

00:07:25.659 --> 00:07:27.809
as a murderous husband in Sudden Fear.

00:07:27.809 --> 00:07:32.749
Sadly, Palance didn't win that year, but he
got a second chance in 1953, earning another

00:07:32.749 --> 00:07:36.280
nomination for his work as a black-clad gunslinger
in Shane.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:40.529
But once again, Palance was robbed of Oscar
glory, and he would have to wait nearly 40

00:07:40.529 --> 00:07:42.240
more years before getting that gold.

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:47.869
Palance would finally get his due thanks to
his performance in City Slickers, a 1991 comedy

00:07:47.869 --> 00:07:51.779
that follows three city dwellers who spend
a vacation at a dude ranch.

00:07:51.779 --> 00:07:55.319
During their quest to become cowboys, they
meet a grizzled old ranch hand by the name

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:56.520
of Curly Washburn.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:58.820
"Did you see that guy?

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:03.000
That's the toughest man I've ever seen in
my life."

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:08.080
Palance is only on screen for around 12 minutes,
but his tough-as-nails performance would nab

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:09.840
him that Best Supporting Actor Oscar.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:15.440
"This man owes you an apology."

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:18.440
Judi Dench is perhaps the most British woman
who's ever lived.

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:21.000
Everything about her just radiates nobility.

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:25.620
So it's no wonder that Dench won an Oscar
for her performance as Queen Elizabeth I in

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:26.680
Shakespeare in Love.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:30.800
Even though she's only onscreen for three
scenes, it's a role she was born to play.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:32.840
Whenever she stands, every knee bows.

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:34.500
She's commanding and demanding.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:37.260
Her humor is biting, and her words cut to
the bone.

00:08:38.420 --> 00:08:39.720
"What do you love so much?"

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:41.720
"Your majesty…"

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:43.580
"Speak up girl, I know who I am."

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:48.040
But beneath that powdered, powerful exterior,
there's a woman with a love for theater and

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:51.970
a sympathy for our star-crossed lovers, William
Shakespeare and Viola.

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:55.740
Alternately hilarious and powerful, Dench
only appears in only eight minutes of the

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:57.830
film, but she owns all of them.

00:08:57.830 --> 00:09:02.570
A complicated royal and a deus ex machina
rolled into one, Dench's Queen Elizabeth is

00:09:02.570 --> 00:09:04.820
worthy of both allegiance and awards.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:07.180
"I thought you were here because you had none."

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:18.220
In 1976, director Sidney Lumet created one
of the darkest, most brilliant satires ever

00:09:18.220 --> 00:09:19.830
put to film: Network.

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:24.310
The film holds up with incredible performances,
but Beatrice Straight made Academy Award history

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:28.540
by winning Best Supporting Actress with just
five minutes of screen time.

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:31.640
Straight plays Louise, the jilted wife of
a TV executive.

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:36.190
When her husband confesses to having an affair
with a younger woman, Louise gives a hell-and-brimstone

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:38.240
sermon that no preacher will ever match.

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:44.020
"I'm your wife dammit, and if you can't work
up a Winter passion for me then the least

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:46.660
I require is respect and allegiance!"

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:51.380
Both embarrassed and filled with righteous
anger, she lays into her husband hard, ripping

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:55.380
him apart for his philandering before breaking
down and admitting how much he's hurting her.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:59.960
And as icing on the acting cake, she ends
the scene actually feeling sorry for her husband,

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:02.720
knowing that his new affair will just leave
him feeling a lot of grief.

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:06.920
All in all, Straight only shows up in Network
for five minutes and 40 seconds.

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:11.530
To this day, no one has beaten Straight's
incredible record, and chances are good she'll

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:14.120
hold onto that honor for a long, long time.

